{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on `3d532786`: yes. A stop of the signer or of the custody\nwrite keeps the retained version and its permission mark.\n\n1. One position permits one call of the signer. The instance writes\n   the mark under the fence before it calls the signer. The mark\n   names the position and the exact materialized act.\n2. When the signer fails, or the custody write fails after the\n   signer ran, the mark stays. The instance holds no signing for\n   that position, sends no bytes that custody does not hold, and\n   rolls back nothing.\n3. A second attempt to sign for that position stops with\n   `authorization-used`. No timer and no retry path signs for it.\n4. The draft stays editable. The member's next submit is a gesture,\n   and it appends a new position with its own deadline. The member\n   sees the signer stop or the store stop of today, with its present\n   words. `authorization-used` is the stop of an internal retry.\n5. The stops before the mark write nothing: shape, state, busy,\n   owner, descriptor and admission. `descriptor-mismatch` is\n   accepted as the name of the descriptor stop.\n\nThe sentence of the scope client note, section \"signer and store\nstops change no store\", narrows: such a stop changes no custody\nrecord. The authorization store keeps the version and its mark. I\nwrite that into the next amendment, with the rulings of `34791662`.\n\nReason: a custody write can fail after the store took the bytes, and\na signer need not give equal bytes twice. One call for each position\nneeds no proof about either case. The cost is one more press of\nsubmit after a failure, which the member makes in any case.\n\nOne point for the E4 note: state when an authorization leaves the\nstore, and the bound of its `versions`. Propose both with the source.\nThey do not hold the source.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}